window.archiveSearchIndex = [{"title":"Heritage","page":"Heritage","href":"heritage.html","text":"Five histories meet in my family. They extend from the Azores and the northwest Iberian borderlands to the North Sea, Santa Catarina and the Río de la Plata."},{"title":"Five strands of family history","page":"Heritage","href":"heritage.html#five-strands-of-family-history","text":"My maternal heritage is predominantly Azorean Portuguese and also includes Galician ancestry. Weber is the surname of my Germanic and Flemish paternal line. Charrúa heritage is another part of my family memory."},{"title":"Ancestry atlas","page":"Heritage","href":"heritage.html#ancestry-atlas","text":"Explore the regions that frame my heritage and the places I study. Markers identify broad heritage regions, historical landmarks and research interests."},{"title":"Family memory and regional history","page":"Heritage","href":"heritage.html#family-memory-and-regional-history","text":"I share broad ancestral connections and the surname Weber. Public historical sources give context for the regions, peoples and cultures that shaped this history."},{"title":"Azorean Heritage","page":"Azorean Heritage","href":"azorean.html","text":"My maternal Portuguese ancestry is predominantly Azorean. The history of Desterro, now Florianópolis, offers regional context for that heritage."},{"title":"Nine islands in the Atlantic","page":"Azorean Heritage","href":"azorean.html#nine-islands-in-the-atlantic","text":"The Azores form three island groups. Flores and Corvo lie to the west. Faial, Pico, São Jorge, Graciosa and Terceira form the central group. São Miguel and Santa Maria lie to the east. The islands’ position mattered to Portuguese Atlantic navigation, and their ports connected island life with ocean trade and imperial shipping."},{"title":"Nossa Senhora do Desterro","page":"Azorean Heritage","href":"azorean.html#nossa-senhora-do-desterro","text":"In 1748 the first group in the organized Azorean settlement of Santa Catarina arrived off Desterro. Further voyages brought island families to communities on and around the Ilha de Santa Catarina, and some settlers eventually moved farther south. These movements form part of the regional history of Azorean heritage in Brazil.Portuguese Crown settlement took place on lands with longstanding Indigenous histories. The vocabulary of “settlement” describes one phase of occupation and should be read alongside that longer history."},{"title":"Island practices in southern Brazil","page":"Azorean Heritage","href":"azorean.html#island-practices-in-southern-brazil","text":"Coastal fishing, vernacular building, religious festivals and bobbin lace help explain the distinctive Azorean presence in the region. These practices changed in Brazil as families adapted to local materials, economies and neighbours. The Casa dos Açores in Biguaçu offers a place to trace that living material culture."},{"title":"Sources for Azorean migration","page":"Azorean Heritage","href":"azorean.html#sources-for-azorean-migration","text":"Public collections in Santa Catarina preserve land grants and passenger material from the colonial era. Read alongside records from the Azores, these sources help researchers distinguish regional settlement patterns from individual voyages."},{"title":"Galician Heritage","page":"Galician Heritage","href":"galician.html","text":"Galicia is part of my maternal family history and a strong cultural and historical interest. The surviving details of that family connection are limited."},{"title":"A connection to Galicia","page":"Galician Heritage","href":"galician.html#a-connection-to-galicia","text":"Galicia is part of my maternal heritage. The borderlands between Galicia and northern Portugal offer wider historical context for studying the region and its languages."},{"title":"A shared medieval language","page":"Galician Heritage","href":"galician.html#a-shared-medieval-language","text":"Northwestern Iberia spans histories of Roman Gallaecia, medieval kingdoms and a long Portuguese–Galician frontier. Medieval Galician-Portuguese lyric travelled across that frontier. Galician and Portuguese developed their own literary histories as their institutions diverged.Galego lives in conversation, publishing, song and education. The nineteenth-century Rexurdimento and the work of writers such as Rosalía de Castro belong to a longer story of public literary renewal. Language and place both matter to Galician identity."},{"title":"Migration and diaspora","page":"Galician Heritage","href":"galician.html#migration-and-diaspora","text":"Brazil was one of several American destinations for migrants from Galicia. The Galician cultural archive documents associations abroad that maintained connections across the Atlantic. Migration records, correspondence and local associations can tell different parts of that history. The general diaspora is context for my interest and remains separate from my own family’s itinerary."},{"title":"Reading a genetic comparison","page":"Galician Heritage","href":"galician.html#reading-a-genetic-comparison","text":"Closeness to a Galician reference in G25 describes proximity within a selected dataset. It cannot establish descent from a particular place or assign a cultural identity.Research on Iberian variation, including a reanalysis of fine-scale patterns within Galicia, illustrates the influence of sample selection and technical batches on apparent clusters."},{"title":"The Weber Line","page":"The Weber Line","href":"germanic.html","text":"Weber is my paternal family surname. The history of German-speaking regions in Europe and southern Brazil gives context to this heritage."},{"title":"Southwestern German territories","page":"The Weber Line","href":"germanic.html#southwestern-german-territories","text":"The Odenwald is an example of the changing historical geography of southwestern Germany. Regions, states and church jurisdictions evolved over time, so the same place can appear under different administrative names in historical sources."},{"title":"The Weser and northern trade","page":"The Weber Line","href":"germanic.html#the-weser-and-northern-trade","text":"The Weser basin and ports such as Bremen are useful for studying trade and settlement in northern German territories. River transport connected inland settlements to North Sea commerce. Hanover and Lower Saxony offer other points of reference for the region’s political history."},{"title":"Santa Catarina","page":"The Weber Line","href":"germanic.html#santa-catarina","text":"German-speaking communities form a distinct part of Santa Catarina’s immigration history. Language, community institutions and historical archives provide ways to study how settlers and their descendants adapted to southern Brazil."},{"title":"Patrilineal inheritance","page":"The Weber Line","href":"germanic.html#patrilineal-inheritance","text":"Y-DNA follows one paternal line. That makes it relevant to some genealogical questions, within a much narrower scope than autosomal ancestry or the family tree as a whole."},{"title":"Flemish Heritage","page":"Flemish Heritage","href":"flemish.html","text":"Flemish-Germanic heritage is part of my paternal family history. Flanders has a distinct history in the Low Countries."},{"title":"Historical Flanders","page":"Flemish Heritage","href":"flemish.html#historical-flanders","text":"The medieval County of Flanders was part of the Low Countries. Its borders shifted and its territory differed from that of the modern Flemish Region. Clothmaking helped cities such as Bruges and Ghent develop large urban markets. Bruges linked Flemish commerce to Hanseatic merchants and other North Sea networks."},{"title":"Language, trade and identity","page":"Flemish Heritage","href":"flemish.html#language-trade-and-identity","text":"Flemish commonly refers to Dutch as used in Flanders and to regional varieties associated with it. Dutch and German both belong to the West Germanic language family. Historical Flanders also contained areas where Romance varieties were spoken. Linguistic kinship and sustained commercial contact are useful contexts for Flemish–German connections. Flemish identity has its own history in the Low Countries."},{"title":"Movement across borders","page":"Flemish Heritage","href":"flemish.html#movement-across-borders","text":"Merchants, artisans and religious communities moved through and out of the Low Countries over several centuries. Trade brought Flemish and German-speaking communities into contact, while political and religious changes also shaped migration. These are broad historical patterns. They provide context for studying Flemish heritage in a wider European setting."},{"title":"My Charrúa Heritage","page":"My Charrúa Heritage","href":"charrua.html","text":"Charrúa heritage is part of my family memory. I explore that connection through the history of the Río de la Plata and the voices of Charrúa descendants."},{"title":"Family memory","page":"My Charrúa Heritage","href":"charrua.html#family-memory","text":"The connection I share here comes from family history. Keeping this heritage visible means learning about Charrúa history, listening to descendants and recognising the continuity of Indigenous communities in the region."},{"title":"Reading the historical term “bugre”","page":"My Charrúa Heritage","href":"charrua.html#reading-the-historical-term-bugre","text":"In Brazil the word bugre was an externally imposed label for Indigenous people and often carried derogatory force. It was applied to different peoples and never supplied the name of one Indigenous nation. I use Charrúa as the specific identification preserved in my family history."},{"title":"Across the Río de la Plata","page":"My Charrúa Heritage","href":"charrua.html#across-the-rio-de-la-plata","text":"Charrúa history belongs to a region that now spans Uruguay and parts of southern Brazil and northeastern Argentina. The Río de la Plata basin and adjoining grasslands supported diverse Indigenous communities, with shifting alliances and ways of living. Today’s borders came later. Colonial records used labels inconsistently, and neighbouring peoples had distinct histories of their own.European colonization brought land seizure, livestock expansion, new trade and warfare. Indigenous people adapted to these changes as they defended territory and negotiated survival. In independent Uruguay, state forces attacked Charrúa people at Salsipuedes in 1831. Survivors faced forced removal, exploitation and marginalization."},{"title":"Survival, memory and continuity","page":"My Charrúa Heritage","href":"charrua.html#survival-memory-and-continuity","text":"Descendants and organizations continue to preserve family memory and press for recognition. Charrúa organizations participated in the campaign to recognize Paso de Salsipuedes as a site of memory. Their own accounts are an essential part of understanding what happened and what survives."},{"title":"Genealogy","page":"Genealogy","href":"genealogy.html","text":"Weber is the family surname shared in this archive. This page brings together broad heritage branches and historical events that help place them in context."},{"title":"Family branches","page":"Genealogy","href":"genealogy.html#family-tree","text":"Expand each branch to explore its cultural and historical context."},{"title":"Regional timeline","page":"Genealogy","href":"genealogy.html#genealogical-timeline","text":"Filter dated events by region. The dates refer to public historical events and objects."},{"title":"Weber heritage in Santa Catarina","page":"Genealogy","href":"genealogy.html#weber-heritage-in-santa-catarina","text":"The Weber surname represents the Germanic and Flemish paternal side of my family history. The cultural history of Santa Catarina provides a wider setting for exploring these connections."},{"title":"Sources and interpretation","page":"Genealogy","href":"genealogy.html#sources-and-interpretation","text":"Family memory and historical sources can illuminate different parts of the past. The public sources here explain regional history and help distinguish it from personal genealogy."},{"title":"About","page":"About","href":"about.html","text":"I’m Brazilian and Muslim. This site brings together the family histories I inherited, the faith I practice and the subjects I keep returning to."},{"title":"Family and place","page":"About","href":"about.html#family-and-place","text":"Santa Catarina is an important point of reference in my family history. My mother’s side carries predominantly Azorean Portuguese ancestry and a Galician connection. The Weber surname belongs to my paternal line, with Germanic and Flemish heritage. My Charrúa heritage also has a place of its own in this archive."},{"title":"Faith and study","page":"About","href":"about.html#faith-and-study","text":"Islam is central to my life. I identify with the Athari creed and strive to follow the Salaf al-Ṣāliḥ. Studying the early generations, Arabic religious terms and the ways narrations were transmitted helps me understand the tradition I seek to follow."},{"title":"Other things I return to","page":"About","href":"about.html#other-things-i-return-to","text":"I’m drawn to historical maps, population genetics, languages and the practical side of computing. Old manuscripts sit comfortably in this collection alongside local AI and games. Counter-Strike 2’s HvH scene and the tropical exploration of Far Cry 3 are very different sides of my interest in digital culture."},{"title":"Islam & My Faith","page":"Islam & My Faith","href":"islam.html","text":"I am Muslim. I identify with the Athari creed, al-ʿAqīdah al-Athariyyah, and strive to follow the Salaf al-Ṣāliḥ as closely as possible."},{"title":"Tawḥīd","page":"Islam & My Faith","href":"islam.html#tawhid","text":"التَّوْحِيدTawḥīd, the affirmation of Allah’s oneness and His exclusive right to worship, is at the heart of Islam. The short sūrah al-Ikhlāṣ is a starting point for reading how the Qur’an expresses divine oneness."},{"title":"The early generations","page":"Islam & My Faith","href":"islam.html#the-early-generations","text":"Salaf al-Ṣāliḥ means the righteous predecessors. The earliest Muslim generations include the Ṣaḥābah, the Companions of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, and the Tābiʿūn, who learned from the Companions. Their place in the transmission of knowledge informs the way I approach Islamic history and religious study."},{"title":"The study of transmitted knowledge","page":"Islam & My Faith","href":"islam.html#the-study-of-transmitted-knowledge","text":"Hadith collections, classical works of creed, scholarly biographies and commentaries preserve different aspects of the Islamic scholarly tradition. Comparing a manuscript page with an edition opens questions about transmission, scribes, and how the book was read across generations."},{"title":"The Athari Creed","page":"The Athari Creed","href":"athari.html","text":"Al-ʿAqīdah al-Athariyyah names the creed I identify with. Its emphasis on revelation and transmitted reports shapes how I approach learning."},{"title":"Revelation and the transmitted tradition","page":"The Athari Creed","href":"athari.html#revelation-and-the-transmitted-tradition","text":"العقيدة الأثريةThe word athar can refer to a transmitted report. In an Athari approach, the Qur’an and authentic Sunnah provide the basis for belief, with particular attention to the understanding of the early Muslims. In questions about Allah’s attributes, a characteristic formulation affirms what revelation says while withholding claims about the modality, bilā kayf."},{"title":"Ahl al-Ḥadīth","page":"The Athari Creed","href":"athari.html#ahl-al-hadith","text":"Ahl al-Ḥadīth designates scholars associated with transmitting, evaluating and drawing on hadith. Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal is an important figure in the history of hadith and traditionalist theology. The label describes a historical scholarly orientation with regional and individual variation."},{"title":"Classical debates","page":"The Athari Creed","href":"athari.html#classical-debates","text":"The ninth-century miḥnah under the Abbasids brought disputes about the createdness of the Qur’an into relation with state authority. Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal’s resistance became a landmark in traditionalist memory. Later debates about divine attributes and the role of kalām, or dialectical theology, produced a wide body of argument and commentary.For a reading path, Aḥmad’s attributed Uṣūl al-Sunnah, al-Barbahārī’s Sharḥ al-Sunnah and Ibn Qudāmah’s Lumʿat al-Iʿtiqād offer examples of different periods and forms of traditionalist creed writing. Editions, attributions and commentary deserve attention when reading them."},{"title":"Early Islamic History","page":"Early Islamic History","href":"islamic-history.html","text":"I’m interested in the relationship between the early Muslim community, the changing political world of the caliphates, and the preservation of religious knowledge."},{"title":"The Prophet ﷺ and his Companions","page":"Early Islamic History","href":"islamic-history.html#the-prophet-and-his-companions","text":"The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ conveyed the Qur’an and taught the early Muslim community. The Hijrah from Mecca to Medina in 622 marks a turning point and provides the epoch of the Islamic calendar. The Companions transmitted what they had heard and seen, and students of the next generation learned from them in different centres."},{"title":"The first caliphates","page":"Early Islamic History","href":"islamic-history.html#the-first-caliphates","text":"632–661 · RashidunThe period of Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān and ʿAlī saw territorial expansion, major changes in government and internal conflict.661–750 · UmayyadsDamascus became the centre of a caliphate extending across a wide range of linguistic and political regions. Arabic assumed a growing role in administration.750–1258 · Abbasids in IraqBaghdad was founded in 762. Court patronage and the movement of scholars and books helped make Iraq a centre of classical Islamic civilization. The sack of Baghdad in 1258 ended the Abbasid caliphate there."},{"title":"A geography of teaching","page":"Early Islamic History","href":"islamic-history.html#a-geography-of-teaching","text":"Medina, Mecca, Kufa, Basra, Damascus and later Baghdad figure in the movement of students and transmitters. The Tābiʿūn and later generations learned in teaching circles, travelled to hear reports, and passed knowledge on by oral instruction and writing. Biographical and hadith works let readers trace parts of those networks.The Ahl al-Ḥadīth and debates over theology belong in this intellectual history alongside political chronology. Collecting reports, weighing their transmission and writing commentaries continued across changes in political rule."},{"title":"Classical Scholarship","page":"Classical Scholarship","href":"scholarship.html","text":"A reading room for Arabic religious literature, the sciences of hadith and the manuscripts that preserve them."},{"title":"Hadith and its sciences","page":"Classical Scholarship","href":"scholarship.html#hadith-and-its-sciences","text":"A hadith has a chain of transmitters, the isnād, and a report text, the matn. Scholars considered continuity, narrator reliability, anomalous wording and hidden defects. Biographical dictionaries preserve information about teachers, students, dates and reliability. Comparing routes of transmission can clarify why one reading or judgement differs from another."},{"title":"Books and commentaries","page":"Classical Scholarship","href":"scholarship.html#books-and-commentaries","text":"Hadith collectionsṢaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī and Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim collect reports and organize them in ways that reward attention to their chapter structures.Creed and methodRead early works of ʿaqīdah alongside historical accounts of their authors and the questions they addressed. Ibn al-Ṣalāḥ’s Muqaddimah offers a later systematic treatment of hadith terminology.Commentary and biographyIbn Ḥajar’s Fatḥ al-Bārī, al-Nawawī’s commentary on Muslim and al-Dhahabī’s Siyar Aʿlām al-Nubalāʾ point toward different ways to read a collection or follow a scholarly life."},{"title":"From codex to digital facsimile","page":"Classical Scholarship","href":"scholarship.html#from-codex-to-digital-facsimile","text":"A manuscript preserves a physical history of copying and reading. Compare colophons, marginal notes, scripts and folio order before treating different copies as interchangeable. Digitization improves access, while shelfmarks, page references and a record of the edition help readers verify quotations.The Qur’an folio shown here is dated 1137 CE and uses eastern Kufic with some elements of naskh. Its catalog record gives the object number and the related colophon evidence. Princeton’s collection offers additional examples across the languages of the Islamic world."},{"title":"Historical Interests","page":"Historical Interests","href":"history.html","text":"I’m drawn to the way maps, archaeology, books and administrative records preserve different accounts of the same places."},{"title":"Europe from antiquity to the Middle Ages","page":"Historical Interests","href":"history.html#europe-from-antiquity-to-the-middle-ages","text":"Roman roads, cities and inscriptions illuminate imperial expansion and local life in Iberia. Later archaeology and written records raise different questions about late antique migrations, Germanic-speaking peoples and medieval societies. Political labels, burial customs, language and biological ancestry can each move at a different pace.In the medieval north, the Hanseatic League connects my interest in Bremen and the Weser to a larger trade network. Flanders adds the history of textiles, urban institutions and North Sea exchange. In Iberia, borderlands and multilingual manuscripts show how cultural institutions operated across political frontiers."},{"title":"Indigenous history and colonial encounters","page":"Historical Interests","href":"history.html#indigenous-history-and-colonial-encounters","text":"I follow the histories of Indigenous peoples in southern Brazil and the Río de la Plata with particular attention to Charrúa history. Archaeological traces, colonial terms, demographic estimates and living community memory have different limits. Accounts of colonization should include displacement, coerced labour and Indigenous decisions and survival."},{"title":"Islamic intellectual history","page":"Historical Interests","href":"history.html#islamic-intellectual-history","text":"The caliphates provide a political chronology, while the history of Ahl al-Ḥadīth and the movement of scholars show how learning travelled. Manuscript catalogues make it possible to follow a text through successive copies and trace part of its reception."},{"title":"Historical map cabinet","page":"Historical Interests","href":"history.html#historical-map-cabinet","text":"Change plates and zoom to examine the linework. Each map reflects the period, knowledge and aims of its maker. The ancestry atlas uses a separate modern basemap for locating places."},{"title":"Population Genetics","page":"Population Genetics","href":"genetics.html","text":"I use population genetics to ask questions about demographic history and to give family research a broader context. Genetic similarity, genealogical descent and cultural identity answer different questions."},{"title":"G25, PCA and Vahaduo","page":"Population Genetics","href":"genetics.html#g25-pca-and-vahaduo","text":"PCA summarizes patterns of variation in a set of data. G25 coordinates provide a 25-dimensional representation used in hobbyist comparisons. A two-axis view can hide differences present on other axes. Vahaduo can compare distances and fit reference mixtures in coordinate space. Distances and model weights depend on the references, coordinate scaling and the model used.A synthetic reference is a calculated average or weighted combination of other references. It can help explore a hypothesis. Its apparent fit does not establish that a discrete historical population with that exact profile existed."},{"title":"Compare coordinates privately","page":"Population Genetics","href":"genetics.html#compare-coordinates-privately","text":"Paste a target and reference rows as label followed by 25 comma-separated coordinates. Use the same G25 scaling for all rows. The explorer ranks full 25-dimensional Euclidean distances and plots axes 1 and 2. Values stay in this browser tab."},{"title":"Ancient Iberia in the published record","page":"Population Genetics","href":"genetics.html#ancient-iberia-in-the-published-record","text":"Olalde and colleagues reported genome-wide data from 271 ancient Iberians across six periods. Select a period to compare coverage and read its context. These are study sample counts."},{"title":"Across Iberia and northern Europe","page":"Population Genetics","href":"genetics.html#across-iberia-and-northern-europe","text":"Iberian research and later work on batch effects show why fine-scale similarities need independent checks. For the Azores, island-level samples and settlement histories matter. A pooled Portuguese reference may obscure differences among islands or between an island and the mainland.Germanic and Flemish population history both intersect with migration, neighbouring communities and regional continuity. Linguistic and historical labels have a different scope from reference sets. Y-DNA describes one direct paternal line, with a much narrower scope than autosomal data."},{"title":"Indigenous ancestry and archaeogenetics","page":"Population Genetics","href":"genetics.html#indigenous-ancestry-and-archaeogenetics","text":"My Charrúa connection comes from family history. Genetic ancestry estimates are a distinct kind of evidence. The ancient genomes studied in Uruguay by Lindo and colleagues document two individuals from one site and raise questions about earlier demographic movements. They cannot stand in for an entire Indigenous people or establish a family relationship."},{"title":"Languages & Linguistics","page":"Languages & Linguistics","href":"languages.html","text":"Portuguese and English are part of the linguistic landscape of this site. Arabic, Galician, Gothic and the historical Germanic languages are particular subjects of interest."},{"title":"Arabic terminology","page":"Languages & Linguistics","href":"languages.html#arabic-terminology","text":"Vocalization and context matter when studying Arabic religious vocabulary. These terms link the language page to the texts and traditions discussed in the Islamic archive.تَوْحِيدTawḥīd · affirmation of Allah’s oneness.عَقِيدَةʿAqīdah · creed.إِسْنَادIsnād · chain of transmission.مَتْنMatn · the report text."},{"title":"Galician and Portuguese","page":"Languages & Linguistics","href":"languages.html#galician-and-portuguese","text":"Galician and Portuguese histories meet in medieval lyric and diverge across later borders and institutions. I’m interested in how modern speakers use related forms, such as Galician noite and Portuguese noite, and how translation handles differences beneath apparent similarity."},{"title":"Gothic and historical Germanic","page":"Languages & Linguistics","href":"languages.html#gothic-and-historical-germanic","text":"Gothic is an attested East Germanic language. The Codex Argenteus preserves much of a Gothic translation of the Gospels in a sixth-century manuscript. Old English, Old High German and Old Norse provide other points of comparison for inflection, regular sound correspondences and inherited vocabulary.In historical linguistics an asterisk can mark a reconstructed form, such as Proto-Germanic *fōt- in comparisons with English foot and Gothic fōtus. A reconstruction is inferred from evidence. A manuscript reading is attested evidence with its own spelling and transmission history."},{"title":"Technology & Computing","page":"Technology & Computing","href":"technology.html","text":"I’m interested in how things work: how browsers render a page, how local models fit into memory, and how a game’s feel depends on the machine running it."},{"title":"A small web","page":"Technology & Computing","href":"technology.html#a-small-web","text":"HTML gives a document its structure, CSS gives it a reading rhythm, and JavaScript adds interaction where it helps. I like the independence of a site that can be kept as files, read offline and moved between hosts. Git records the history of changes, while GitHub can serve as a place to keep and publish them.Python is useful for working with source lists, cleaning tabular data and exploring historical or genetic datasets. The question that interests me is how to make the result easy for someone else to inspect."},{"title":"Local AI and model formats","page":"Technology & Computing","href":"technology.html#local-ai-and-model-formats","text":"Running large language models locally brings the trade-offs into view. Ollama offers a local runtime, and GGUF packages weights and model metadata in an ecosystem commonly used for quantized inference. Memory requirements depend on the weights, context length and runtime. Comparing output quality, tokens per second and memory use gives a fuller picture than comparing model file sizes alone."},{"title":"Hardware and gaming technology","page":"Technology & Computing","href":"technology.html#hardware-and-gaming-technology","text":"CPU scheduling, GPU memory, cooling, storage and display refresh affect different workloads in different ways. For games, frame pacing and input latency can matter as much as average frame rate. Software experiments are most informative when a change is isolated and the same scenario can be repeated."},{"title":"Gaming & Digital Culture","page":"Gaming & Digital Culture","href":"gaming.html","text":"Counter-Strike 2’s HvH scene and Far Cry 3’s island atmosphere are two very different reasons games belong in this archive."},{"title":"Counter-Strike 2 · Hack vs Hack","page":"Gaming & Digital Culture","href":"gaming.html#counter-strike-2-hack-vs-hack","text":"My main interest in CS2 is HvH, or Hack vs Hack. It is a subculture of custom environments where participants expect modified play. Its communities build a shared vocabulary around configurations, rules, server styles, map habits and the way different updates change the scene.Configuration culture includes the sharing and comparison of presets and preferences. The technical questions I follow include how client and server state diverge, how tick timing and latency affect what players observe, and how custom server rules reshape movement and positioning. Map geometry, peeking and round pacing remain part of how players read a match. These interests sit alongside ordinary Counter-Strike customization, including HUDs, visual settings and binds."},{"title":"Far Cry 3 · The Rook Islands","page":"Gaming & Digital Culture","href":"gaming.html#far-cry-3-the-rook-islands","text":"Tropical shorelines, dense vegetation and steep interior ridges make exploration central to the game’s atmosphere. The contrast between a clear coastal horizon and short sightlines under the canopy makes moving around the fictional Rook Islands its own pleasure. The photograph above shows Wayag in Indonesia as a visual companion to the game’s setting."},{"title":"The collection","page":"Gaming & Digital Culture","href":"gaming.html#the-collection","text":"FortniteA changing play space shaped by movement and player creativity.Grand Theft Auto VFree exploration through a dense city and its surrounding countryside.Rainbow Six SiegeDestructible environments and precise use of space.Red Dead Redemption 2Slow travel, landscape and attention to everyday detail.Dying LightParkour routes and the pressure of a changing day–night cycle.The ForestSurvival, shelter building and uneasy exploration.Watch Dogs 2An open-world take on digital culture and urban infrastructure."},{"title":"Sources & image credits","page":"Archive","href":"sources.html","text":"Manuscripts, maps, licenses, visual credits."}];
